From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: a repeated handover signal is not an error

Every audio DSP stop opens with an error that describes normal SMP2P
behaviour:

    # echo stop > /sys/class/remoteproc/remoteproc0/state
    qcom_q6v5_pas 6800000.remoteproc: Handover signaled, but it already happened

fatal, ready, handover and stop-ack are four bits of one inbound SMP2P entry,
and qcom_smp2p_notify_in() raises an interrupt for a bit whose value differs
from entry->last_value. Once the SSR-ack feature has been negotiated,
qcom_smp2p_check_ssr() zeroes last_value on *every* scan, not only on a
restart, so each doorbell computes its edges against zero and re-delivers
every bit the remote still has set.

The doorbell in question is the remote acknowledging the stop request this
driver just made. ready, handover and stop-ack are all still asserted at that
point, so all three are delivered: stop-ack is the one being waited for, ready
merely completes an already-complete completion, and handover lands in a
handler that has nothing left to do, because the proxy regulators, clocks and
power domains were dropped when it first fired.

The guard is right -- calling ->handover() twice would be an unbalanced
disable of all of those -- but the condition it guards against is routine
here, not a misbehaving remote. Demote the message.

Deliberately not fixed in SMP2P, where the unconditional reset arguably
belongs inside the restart check: on this SoC the resulting re-delivery is
what raises the ready edge after a restart at all. Making it conditional risks
every start falling back to 0084's line-level path and paying a full 5 s
timeout, for no gain beyond this one log line.

Not hibernation-specific: a plain SSR reproduces it, and the hibernate path
merely performs a stop/start.

---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -229,7 +229,16 @@
 	struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5 = data;
 
 	if (q6v5->handover_issued) {
-		dev_err(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
+		/*
+		 * Not an error, and routine on SMP2P: once the SSR-ack feature
+		 * is negotiated, qcom_smp2p_check_ssr() drops the cached copy
+		 * of every inbound entry on each scan, so the next doorbell --
+		 * the remote acknowledging a stop, for one -- computes its
+		 * edges against zero and re-delivers every bit the remote
+		 * still has set, handover included. The proxy resources were
+		 * released the first time round; there is nothing left to do.
+		 */
+		dev_dbg(q6v5->dev, "handover signaled again, already handled\n");
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
